== The Mind Enlightened

IV. Gracious affections arise from the mind’s being enlightened to understand or apprehend divine things rightly and spiritually.

Holy affections are not heat without light. They always arise from some information of the understanding, some spiritual instruction the mind receives, some light or actual knowledge. The child of God is graciously affected because he sees and understands something more of divine things than he did before: more of God or Christ, and of the glorious things set forth in the gospel. He has some clearer and better view than he had before, when he was not affected. Either he receives some understanding of divine things that is new to him, or his former knowledge is renewed after the view of it had decayed. 1 John 4:7: “Everyone who loves … knows God.” Phil 1:9: “that your#super[+] love may abound more and more in knowledge and depth of insight.” Rom 10:2: “they are zealous for God, but not on the basis of knowledge.” Col 3:10: “the new self, which is being renewed in knowledge.” Ps 43:3–4: “Send out Your light and Your truth; let them lead me. Let them bring me to Your holy mountain.” John 6:45: “It is written in the Prophets: ‘And they will all be taught by God.’ Everyone who has heard the Father and learned from Him comes to Me.” Knowledge is the key that first opens the hard heart and enlarges the affections, and so opens the way for men into the kingdom of heaven. Luke 11:52: “You#super[+] have taken away the key to knowledge.”

Now there are many affections which do not arise from any light in the understanding. And when this is so, it is a sure evidence that these affections are not spiritual, however high they may be#footnote[Many who have had very strong affections at first conversion afterward become dry, and wither, waste, pine and die away. And now their hypocrisy is manifest: if not to all the world by open profaneness, yet to the discerning eye of living Christians, by a formal, barren, unsavory, unfruitful heart and course; because they never yet had light enough to convict them.—It is strange to see some people carried along with mighty affection against sin and hell, and after Christ. And what is the hell you fear? A dreadful place. What is Christ? They hardly know as much as the devils do; but that is all. Oh, do not trust them! Many #emph[have] fallen away, and these #emph[will] fall away, to some lust, or opinion, or pride, or the world; and the reason is that they never had light enough. John 5:35: John #emph[was a burning and shining light, and they rejoiced in him for a season;] yet glorious as it was, they did not see Christ by it, especially not with divine light. It is rare to see Christians full of both light and affection. And so consider this. Many a man has been well brought up, and is of a sweet, loving nature, mild and gentle and harmless; he likes and loves the best things; his meaning and mind and heart are good, and he has more in his heart than in show; and so he hopes all will go well with him. I say there may be the greatest hypocrisy under the greatest affections, especially if they lack light. You will be hardened in your hypocrisy by them. I never liked violent affections and pangs, but only such as were dropped in by light; because those come from an external principle and do not last, but these do. Men are not frightened by the light of the sun, though it is clearer than the lightning. #emph[Shepard’s] parable Part I. P. 146.] . Indeed they have some new apprehensions which they did not have before. Such is the nature of man that it is impossible for his mind to be affected except by something he apprehends, or that his mind conceives of. But in many persons the apprehensions or conceptions they are affected by have nothing of the nature of knowledge or instruction in them. For instance, a person may be affected by a lively idea, suddenly stirred up in his mind, of some shape, or a very beautiful, pleasant face, or some shining light, or some other glorious outward appearance. Here something is apprehended or conceived by the mind, but there is nothing of the nature of instruction in it. Persons become none the wiser by such things, nor more knowing about God, or a Mediator between God and man, or the way of salvation by Christ, or anything contained in any of the doctrines of the gospel. By these external ideas persons gain no further acquaintance with God in any of the attributes or perfections of his nature, nor any further understanding of his word, or of any of his ways or works. Truly spiritual and gracious affections are not raised in this way. They arise from the enlightening of the understanding to understand the things that are taught of God and Christ in a new way: the coming to a new understanding of the excellent nature of God and his wonderful perfections, some new view of Christ in his spiritual excellences and fullness, or things belonging to the way of salvation by Christ opened to a person in a new way, so that he now sees how it is, and understands those divine and spiritual doctrines which were once foolishness to him. Such enlightenings of the understanding as these are things entirely different in their nature from strong ideas of shapes and colors, outward brightness and glory, or sounds and voices. So the fact that all gracious affections arise from some instruction or enlightening of the understanding is a further proof that affections arising from such impressions on the imagination are not gracious affections, besides the things observed before which make this evident.

From this it also appears that affections arising from texts of Scripture coming to the mind are vain when the ground of the affection is not any instruction received in the understanding from those texts, or anything taught in them, but the way they came to the mind. When Christ makes the Scripture a means of the heart’s burning with gracious affection, it is #emph[by opening the Scriptures to their understandings.] Luke 24:32: “Were not our hearts burning within us as He spoke with us on the road and opened the Scriptures to us?” It also appears that an affection occasioned by the coming of a text of Scripture must be vain when it is founded on something supposed to be taught by the text which is really not contained in it, nor in any other Scripture. For such supposed instruction is not real instruction, but a mistake and misapprehension of the mind. For instance, persons may suppose that they are expressly taught by some Scripture coming to their minds that they in particular are loved by God, or that their sins are forgiven, that God is their Father, and the like. This is a mistake or misapprehension. For the Scripture nowhere reveals expressly which individual persons are loved, but only by consequence, by revealing the qualifications of persons who are loved by God. So this matter is not to be learned from Scripture in any other way than by consequence, from these qualifications; for things are not to be #emph[learned from] Scripture in any other way than they are #emph[taught in] Scripture.

In the instances mentioned, affections really arise from ignorance rather than instruction; and so too in some others that might be mentioned. Some, when they find themselves free of speech in prayer, call it God’s being with them; this affects them more, and so their affections are set going and increased. Yet they do not look into the cause of this freedom of speech, which may arise in many other ways besides God’s spiritual presence. So some are much affected by some apt thoughts about Scripture that come into their minds, and call it the Spirit of God teaching them. So they ascribe many of the workings of their own minds, which they have a high opinion of and are pleased and taken with, to the special, immediate influences of God’s Spirit, and so are mightily affected by their privilege. And there are some instances of persons in whom the first ground of their affection seems clearly to be some bodily sensation. The animal spirits, from some cause (and probably sometimes by the devil), are suddenly and unaccountably put into a very agreeable motion, causing persons to feel pleasant sensations in their bodies. The animal spirits are put into such a motion as is usually connected with the exhilaration of the mind, and the soul, by the laws of the union of soul and body, feels pleasure from it. The motion of the animal spirits does not first arise from any affection or apprehension of the mind whatever. The very first thing that is felt is an exhilaration of the animal spirits and a pleasant external sensation, perhaps in the breast. Then, through ignorance, the person is taken by surprise and begins to think, Surely this is the Holy Spirit coming into me. And then the mind begins to be affected and raised. First there is great joy, and then many other affections, in a very tumultuous way, throwing all nature, both body and mind, into a mighty commotion. For though, as I observed before, it is the soul alone that is #emph[the seat of the affections,] this does not prevent bodily sensations from being, in this way, #emph[an occasion of affections] in the mind.

And even if men’s religious affections do truly arise from some instruction or light in the understanding, the affection is still not gracious unless the light which is its ground is spiritual. Affections may be stirred up by the understanding of things that men obtain merely by human teaching, with the common use and development of the faculties of the mind. Men may be much affected by knowledge of the things of religion that they obtain in this way, as some philosophers have been mightily affected, and almost carried beyond themselves, by the discoveries they have made in mathematics and natural philosophy. So men may be much affected by common illuminations of the Spirit of God, in which God assists men’s faculties to a greater degree of the kind of understanding of religious matters which they have in some degree by the ordinary exercise and development of their own faculties alone. Such illuminations may much affect the mind, as in many we read of in Scripture #emph[who were once enlightened;] but these affections are not spiritual.

If the Scriptures are of any use to teach us anything, there is such a thing as a spiritual, supernatural understanding of divine things which is peculiar to the saints, and of which those who are not saints have nothing. The apostle certainly speaks of a kind of understanding, apprehending or discerning of divine things which natural men have nothing of, in 1 Cor 2:14: “The natural man does not accept the things that come from the Spirit of God. For they are foolishness to him, and he cannot understand them, because they are spiritually discerned.” A kind of seeing or discerning of spiritual things peculiar to the saints is certainly spoken of in 1 John 3:6: “No one who continues to sin has seen Him or known Him.” 3 John 11: “The one who does evil has not seen God.” And John 6:40: “It is My Father’s will that everyone who sees the Son and believes in Him shall have eternal life.” John 14:19: “The world will see Me no more, but you#super[+] will see Me.” John 17:3: “Now this is eternal life, that they may know You, the only true God, and Jesus Christ, whom You have sent.” Matt 11:27: “No one knows the Son except the Father, and no one knows the Father except the Son and those to whom the Son chooses to reveal Him.” John 12:45: “Whoever sees Me sees the One who sent Me.” Ps 9:10: “Those who know Your name trust in You.” Phil 3:8: “I count all things as loss compared to the surpassing excellence of knowing Christ Jesus my Lord …” Verse 10: “I want to know Christ …” And there are countless other places all over the Bible which show the same. And that there is such a thing as an understanding of divine things which in its nature and kind is wholly different from all the knowledge natural men have is evident from this: there is an understanding of divine things which Scripture calls spiritual understanding. Col 1:9: “We have not stopped praying for you#super[+] and asking God to fill you#super[+] with the knowledge of His will in all spiritual wisdom and understanding.” It has already been shown that what is #emph[spiritual,] in the ordinary use of the word in the New Testament, is entirely different in nature and kind from all that natural men experience or can experience.

From this it may be inferred with certainty what spiritual understanding consists in. For if there is in the saints a kind of apprehension or perception which in its nature is entirely different from all that natural men have, or can possibly have until they have a new nature, it must consist in their having a certain kind of ideas or sensations of mind which are simply different from all that is or can be in the minds of natural men. And that is the same as saying that it consists in the sensations of a new spiritual sense, which the souls of natural men do not have, as is evident from what has been observed again and again before. But I have already shown what that new spiritual sense is which the saints are given in regeneration, and what its object is. I have shown that its immediate object is the supreme beauty and excellence of the nature of divine things as they are in themselves. And this agrees with Scripture. The apostle very plainly teaches that the great thing shown by spiritual light, and understood by spiritual knowledge, is the glory of divine things. 2 Cor 4:3–4: “And even if our gospel is veiled, it is veiled to those who are perishing. The god of this age has blinded the minds of unbelievers so they cannot see the light of the gospel of the glory of Christ, who is the image of God.” Together with verse 6: “For God, who said, ‘Let light shine out of darkness,’ made His light shine in our hearts to give us the light of the knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ.” And 2 Cor 3:18, just before: “And we, who with unveiled faces all behold the glory of the Lord, are being transformed into His image with intensifying glory, which comes from the Lord, who is the Spirit.” And it must be so. For, as has been observed before, Scripture often teaches that all true religion consists, in sum, in the love of divine things. So the kind of understanding or knowledge which is the proper foundation of true religion must be the knowledge of the #emph[loveliness of divine things.] For no doubt the knowledge which is the proper foundation of #emph[love] is the knowledge of #emph[loveliness.] What that beauty or loveliness of divine things is which is the proper and immediate object of a spiritual sense of mind was shown under the last head discussed: namely, that it is the beauty of their moral perfection. So it is in the view or sense of this that spiritual understanding most immediately and primarily consists. And indeed it is plain that it can be nothing else. For (as has been shown) natural men and devils can see and know everything belonging to divine things except the beauty of their moral excellence and those properties and qualities of divine things of which this beauty is the foundation; and all the rest they will know fully and clearly to all eternity.

From what has been said, then, we necessarily come to this conclusion about what spiritual understanding consists in: namely, that it consists in #emph[a sense of the heart of the supreme beauty and sweetness of the holiness or moral perfection of divine things, together with all the discerning and knowledge of the things of religion that depends on, and flows from, such a sense.]

Spiritual understanding consists primarily in #emph[a sense of heart of that spiritual beauty.] I say, a #emph[sense of heart;] for it is not merely speculation that is involved in this kind of understanding. Nor can a clear distinction be made in this matter between the two faculties of understanding and will, as acting distinctly and separately. When the mind has a sense of the sweet beauty and loveliness of a thing, that implies a sense of sweetness and delight in the presence of the idea of it. And this sense of the loveliness or delightfulness of beauty carries in its very nature #emph[the sense of the heart:] an effect and impression the soul receives as a substance possessed of taste, inclination and will.

There is a distinction to be made between a merely #emph[notional understanding,] in which the mind only beholds things in the exercise of a speculative faculty, and #emph[the sense of the heart,] in which the mind does not only #emph[speculate] and #emph[behold,] but #emph[relishes] and #emph[feels.] The sort of knowledge by which a man has a felt perception of loveliness and loathsomeness, or of sweetness and nauseousness, is not the same sort of knowledge as that by which he knows what a triangle is and what a square is. The one is merely speculative knowledge; the other is sensible knowledge, a knowledge of feeling, in which more than the mere intellect is involved. The heart is its proper subject: the soul as a being that not only beholds but has inclination, and is pleased or displeased. And yet there is the nature of instruction in it; as he who has perceived the sweet taste of honey knows much more about it than he who has only looked at it and touched it.

The apostle seems to make a distinction between merely speculative knowledge of the things of religion and spiritual knowledge, in calling the former “the form of knowledge and truth.” Rom 2:20: “because you have in the law the form of knowledge and truth.” The latter is often represented by relishing, smelling or tasting. 2 Cor 2:14: “But thanks be to God, who always leads us triumphantly as captives in Christ and through us spreads everywhere the fragrance of the knowledge of Him.” Matt 16:23: “You do not savor the things of God, but the things of men.” 1 Pet 2:2–3: “Like newborn babies, crave pure spiritual milk, so that by it you#super[+] may grow up in your#super[+] salvation, now that you#super[+] have tasted that the Lord is good.” Song 1:3: “The #emph[fragrance] of your perfume is pleasing; #emph[your name is like perfume poured out.] No wonder the maidens adore you”; compared with 1 John 2:20: “You#super[+], however, have an #emph[anointing] from the Holy One, and all of you#super[+] know the truth.”

Spiritual understanding consists primarily in this #emph[sense, or taste, of the moral beauty of divine things;] so that no knowledge can be called spiritual except so far as it arises from this and has this in it. But #emph[secondarily] it includes #emph[all the discerning and knowledge of the things of religion that depends on, and flows from, such a sense.]

When the true beauty and loveliness of the holiness, or true moral good, that is in divine things is shown to the soul, it opens, as it were, a new world to its view. This shows the glory of all the perfections of God, and of everything belonging to the divine being; for, as was observed before, the beauty of all arises from God’s moral perfection. This shows the glory of all God’s works, both of creation and of providence. For it is their special glory that God’s holiness, righteousness, faithfulness and goodness are so displayed in them; and without these moral perfections there would be no glory in the power and skill with which they are done. The glorifying of God’s moral perfections is the special end of all the works of God’s hands. By this sense of the moral beauty of divine things the sufficiency of Christ as a mediator is understood. For it is only by the showing of the beauty of the moral perfection of Christ that the believer is let into the knowledge of the excellence of his person, so as to know anything more of it than the devils do. And it is only by the knowledge of the excellence of Christ’s person that anyone knows his sufficiency as a mediator, for the latter depends on and arises from the former. It is by seeing the excellence of Christ’s person that the saints are brought to a sense of the preciousness of his blood and its sufficiency to atone for sin. For the preciousness of Christ’s blood consists in this: that it is the blood of so excellent and lovely a person. And on this depend the merit of his obedience and the sufficiency and prevailing power of his intercession. By this sight of the moral beauty of divine things the beauty of the way of salvation by Christ is seen. For that consists in the beauty of the moral perfections of God, which shines out wonderfully in every step of this method of salvation, from beginning to end. By this the fitness and suitableness of this way is seen. For this consists wholly in its tendency to deliver us from sin and hell, and to bring us to the happiness which consists in the possession and enjoyment of moral good, in a way that sweetly agrees with God’s moral perfections. And the excellent wisdom of that way consists in its being so designed as to attain these ends. By this the excellence of the word of God is seen. Take away all the moral beauty and sweetness in the word, and the Bible is left wholly a dead letter, a dry, lifeless, tasteless thing. By this the true foundation of our duty is seen: God’s worthiness to be esteemed, honored, loved, submitted to and served as he requires of us, and the loveliness of the duties themselves that are required of us. And by this the true evil of sin is seen; for he who sees the beauty of holiness must necessarily see the hatefulness of sin, its contrary. By this men understand the true glory of heaven, which consists in the beauty and happiness that is in holiness. By this the loveliness and happiness of both saints and angels is seen. He who sees the beauty of holiness, or true moral good, sees the greatest and most important thing in the world, which is the fullness of all things, without which all the world is empty, no better than nothing; indeed, worse than nothing. Unless this is seen, nothing is seen that is worth seeing; for there is no other true excellence or beauty. Unless this is understood, nothing is understood that is worthy of the exercise of the noble faculty of understanding. This is the beauty of the Godhead, and the divinity of divinity (if I may speak so), the good of the infinite fountain of good; without which God himself (if that were possible) would be an infinite evil; without which it would have been better for us never to have existed; and without which it would have been better for there to be no being at all. So the man who does not know this in effect knows nothing. His knowledge is only the shadow of knowledge, or the form of knowledge, as the apostle calls it. Well, then, may Scripture represent those who lack that spiritual sense by which the beauty of holiness is perceived as totally blind, deaf and senseless, indeed dead. And well may regeneration, in which this divine sense is given to the soul by its Creator, be represented as opening blind eyes, raising the dead, and bringing a person into a new world. For if what has been said is considered, it will be clear that when a person is given this sense and knowledge, he will view nothing as he did before. Though before he #emph[knew] all things #emph[according to the flesh, from now on he will know them so no longer;] and #emph[he is a new creation; the old has passed away; all things have become new;] in agreement with 2 Corinthians 5:16–17.

And besides the things already mentioned, all true experiential knowledge of religion arises from this sense of spiritual beauty; and this is in itself, as it were, a new world of knowledge. He who does not see the beauty of holiness does not know what a single one of the graces of God’s Spirit is. He has no idea or conception of any gracious exercises of soul, any holy comforts and delights, or any effects of the saving influences of the Spirit of God on the heart. And so he is ignorant of the greatest works of God, the most important and glorious effects of his power on the creature. He is also wholly ignorant of the saints as saints; he does not know what they are. In effect he is ignorant of the whole spiritual world.

Since things are so, it plainly appears that God’s implanting the spiritual, supernatural sense that has been spoken of makes a great change in a man. Were it not for the very imperfect degree in which this sense is commonly given at first, or the small degree of this glorious light that first dawns on the soul, the change made by this spiritual opening of the eyes in conversion would be much greater, and more remarkable in every way, than if a man born blind, with only the other four senses, should remain so for a long time, and then should suddenly be given the sense of sight in the midst of the clear light of the sun, showing him a world of visible objects. For though sight is more noble than any of the other external senses, this spiritual sense which has been spoken of is infinitely more noble than that, or than any other principle of discerning that a man naturally has; and the object of this sense is infinitely greater and more important.

This sort of understanding or knowledge is the knowledge of divine things from which all truly gracious affections proceed; and so it is by this that all affections are to be tested. Those affections that arise wholly from any other kind of knowledge, or result from any other kind of apprehensions of mind, are vain. #footnote[Take care not to content yourselves with every kind of knowledge. Do not worship every image of your own heads; especially you who fall short of the truth, or of the knowledge of it. For when you have some, there may still be something lacking which would make you sincere. There are many men of great knowledge, able to teach themselves and others too, and yet their hearts are unsound. How does this come about? Is it because they have so much light? No, but because they lack much. And so do not content yourselves with every knowledge. There is some knowledge which men have by the light of nature, from the book of creation (which leaves them without excuse); some by the power of education; some by the light of the law, by which men know their sin and evils; some by the letter of the gospel; and so men may know much and speak well, and so in seeing not see; some by the Spirit, and they may see much, so as to prophesy in Christ’s name, and yet be told to depart (Matt 7). Now there is #emph[a light of glory] by which the elect see things in another way; how, they cannot tell you. It is the beginning of the light of heaven: the same Spirit that fills Christ filling their minds, so that they know all things by this anointing. If you ever have this, you must become babes and fools in your own eyes. God will never write his law in your minds until all the scribblings of it are blotted out. Count all your knowledge loss for the gaining of this. It is sad to see many a man pleasing himself in his own dreaming delusions; yet the poor creature in seeing does not see. This is God’s heavy curse on men under the greatest means, and it lays all waste and desolate. #emph[Shepard]’s parable, Part I. P. 147.]

From what has been said we may learn where the most essential difference lies between the light or understanding given by the common influences of the Spirit of God on the hearts of natural men and the saving instruction given to the saints. The latter lies primarily and most essentially in beholding the holy beauty that is in divine things, which is the only #emph[true] moral good, and to which the soul of fallen man is by nature totally blind. The former consists only in a further understanding, through the assistance of natural principles, of those things which men may know in some measure by the ordinary exercise of their faculties alone. And this knowledge consists only in the knowledge of those things belonging to religion which are #emph[natural.] So, for instance, in the awakenings and convictions of conscience that natural men are often subject to, the Spirit of God gives no knowledge of the true #emph[moral] beauty which is in divine things. He only assists the mind to a clearer idea of the guilt of sin, or its relation to a punishment and its connection with the #emph[evil of suffering] (without any sight of its true #emph[moral] evil, or its odiousness as sin), and to a clearer idea of the #emph[natural] perfections of God, in which consists not his holy beauty and glory but his fearful and terrible greatness. It is a clear sight of this that will fully awaken the consciences of wicked men at the day of judgment, #emph[without any spiritual light.] And it is a lesser degree of the same that awakens the consciences of natural men in this world, #emph[without spiritual light.] The same discoveries are given in some measure in the conscience of an awakened sinner in this world which will be given more fully in the consciences of sinners at the day of judgment. The same kind of sight or apprehension of God which, in a lesser degree, makes awakened sinners in this world feel the dreadful guilt of sin against so great and terrible a God, and feel its amazing punishment, and fills them with fearful apprehensions of divine wrath, is what will thoroughly convince all wicked men of the infinitely dreadful nature and guilt of sin, and astonish them with apprehensions of wrath, when Christ comes in the glory of his power and majesty, and every eye sees him, and all the tribes of the earth mourn because of him. And in the common illuminations sometimes given to natural men, stirring up in them some kind of religious desire, love and joy, the mind is only assisted to a clearer apprehension of the #emph[natural good] that is in divine things. So at times, under common illuminations, men are elated by the ideas of the natural good that is in heaven: its outward glory, its ease, its honor and advancement, being there the objects of the high favor of God and the great regard of men and angels, and so on. So there are many things set forth in the gospel concerning God and Christ and the way of salvation that have a #emph[natural] good in them, which suits the natural principle of self-love. So in the great goodness of God to sinners, and the wonderful dying love of Christ, there is a natural good which all men love as they love themselves, as well as a spiritual and holy beauty which is seen only by the regenerate. So there are many things belonging to the word of God’s grace delivered in the gospel which may cause natural men, when they hear it, #emph[at once to receive it with joy.] All the love natural men have for God and Christ, for Christian virtues and good men, comes not from any sight of the loveliness of the holiness, or true #emph[moral] excellence, of these things, but only for the sake of the #emph[natural] good there is in them. All natural men’s hatred of sin comes as much from principles of nature as men’s hatred of a tiger for his ferocity, or their aversion to a snake for its poison and harmfulness. And all their love of Christian virtue comes from no higher principle than their love of a man’s good nature, which appears lovely to natural men; but no otherwise than silver and gold appear lovely in the eyes of a merchant, or the blackness of the soil is beautiful in the eyes of the farmer.

From what has been said of the nature of spiritual understanding, it appears that spiritual understanding does not consist in any new doctrinal knowledge, or in having any new proposition suggested to the mind which was not read or heard of before. For it is plain that this suggesting of new propositions is a thing entirely different from giving the mind a new taste or relish of beauty and sweetness. #footnote[#emph[Calvin,] in his institutions, book I. Chap. Ix. § 1, says that it is not the office of the Spirit who is promised to us to make new revelations, unheard of before, or to coin some new kind of doctrine which tends to draw us away from the received doctrine of the gospel, but to seal and confirm to us that very doctrine which is by the gospel. And in the same place he speaks of some who in those days held the contrary notion, #emph[claiming to be immediately led by the Spirit, as persons governed by a most haughty self-conceit, and not so properly to be looked on as only laboring under a mistake as driven by a sort of raving madness.]] It is also evident that spiritual knowledge does not consist in any new doctrinal explanation of any part of Scripture. For this is still only doctrinal knowledge, or the knowledge of propositions. The doctrinal explaining of any part of Scripture only gives us to understand what propositions are contained or taught in that part of Scripture.

So it appears that the #emph[spiritual understanding] of Scripture does not consist in opening to the mind the mystical meaning of Scripture in its parables, types and allegories; for this is only a doctrinal explanation of Scripture. He who explains what is meant by the stony ground, and the seed’s springing up suddenly and quickly withering away, only explains what propositions or doctrines are taught in it. So he who explains what is typified by #emph[Jacob]’s ladder, and the angels of God ascending and descending on it, or what was typified by #emph[Joshua]’s leading #emph[Israel] through the #emph[Jordan,] only shows what propositions are hidden in these passages. And many men can explain these types who have no spiritual knowledge. A man might know how to interpret all the types, parables, riddles and allegories in the Bible and not have one beam of spiritual light in his mind. For he may not have the least degree of that spiritual sense of the holy beauty of divine things which has been spoken of, and may see nothing of this kind of glory in anything contained in any of these mysteries, or in any other part of Scripture. It is plain from what the apostle says that a man might understand all such mysteries and have no saving grace. 1 Cor 13:2: “If I have the gift of prophecy and can fathom all mysteries and all knowledge … but have not love, I am nothing.” So those are very foolish who are exalted in an opinion of their own spiritual attainments by notions that come into their minds of the mystical meaning of this or that passage of Scripture, as though this were a spiritual understanding of these passages given them immediately by the Spirit of God, and who have their affections highly raised by it. What has been said shows the vanity of such affections.

From what has been said it is also evident that it is not spiritual knowledge for persons to be informed of their duty by having it immediately suggested to their minds that such and such outward actions or deeds are the will of God. If we suppose that it truly is God’s way to signify his will to his people like this, by immediate inward suggestions, such suggestions have nothing of the nature of spiritual light. Such knowledge would be only one kind of doctrinal knowledge. A proposition concerning the will of God is as properly a doctrine of religion as a proposition concerning the nature of God, or a work of God. And having either of these kinds of propositions, or any other proposition, declared to a man, either by speech or by inward suggestion, differs vastly from having the holy beauty of divine things shown to the soul, in which spiritual knowledge most essentially consists. So there was no spiritual light in #emph[Balaam,] though he had the will of God immediately suggested to him by the Spirit of God from time to time, concerning the way he should go and what he should do and say.

It is clear, then, that being led and directed in this way is not that holy and spiritual #emph[leading of the Spirit of God] which is peculiar to the saints, and a distinguishing mark of the sons of God, spoken of in Romans 8:14: “For all who are led by the Spirit of God are sons of God.” Gal 5:18: “But if you#super[+] are led by the Spirit, you#super[+] are not under the law.”

And suppose persons have the will of God concerning their actions suggested to them by some text of Scripture suddenly and extraordinarily brought to their minds. As the words stood in the Bible before they came to their minds, the text related to the actions and behavior of some other person; but they suppose that, as God sent the words to them, he meant something further by them, and meant some particular action of theirs. I say, if persons should have the will of God suggested to them with texts of Scripture in this way, it does not alter the case. The suggestion’s being accompanied by an apt text of Scripture does not make it of the nature of spiritual instruction. For instance, suppose a person in #emph[New England] were on some occasion at a loss whether it was his duty to go into some popish or heathen land, where he was likely to be exposed to many difficulties and dangers. He prays to God to show him the way of his duty; and after earnest prayer he has the words God spoke to #emph[Jacob] in #emph[Genesis] 46 suddenly and extraordinarily brought to his mind, as if they were spoken to him: “Do not be afraid to go down to Egypt … I will go down with you to Egypt, and I will surely bring you back.” As these words stood in the Bible before they came to his mind, they related only to #emph[Jacob] and his conduct. Yet he supposes that God has a further meaning in them, as they were brought and applied to him: that they are to be understood in a new sense, that by #emph[Egypt] is meant this particular country he has in mind, that the action intended is his going there, and that the meaning of the promise is that God will bring him back to #emph[New England] again. There is nothing of the nature of a spiritual or gracious leading of the Spirit in this, for there is nothing of the nature of spiritual understanding in it. To understand texts of Scripture in this way is not to have a spiritual understanding of them. To understand Scripture spiritually is to understand rightly what #emph[is in] Scripture, and what #emph[was in] it before it was understood. It is to understand rightly what #emph[was always contained in] its meaning, and not to make a new meaning. When the mind is enlightened to understand Scripture spiritually and rightly, it is enabled to see in Scripture what was not seen before #emph[because of blindness.] But if it was because of blindness, that is evidence that the same meaning was in it before; otherwise it would have been no blindness not to see it. It is no blindness not to see a meaning which is not there. Spiritually enlightening the eyes to understand Scripture is #emph[opening the eyes.] Ps 119:18: “Open my eyes that I may see wondrous things from Your law.” This shows that the reason the same thing was not seen in Scripture before was that the eyes were #emph[shut.] That would not be the case if the meaning now understood was not there before, but is now newly added to Scripture by the way the Scripture came to my mind. This making of a new meaning for Scripture is the same thing as making a new Scripture. It is properly adding to the word, which is threatened with so dreadful a curse. To understand Scripture spiritually is to have the eyes of the mind opened to behold the wonderful spiritual excellence of the glorious things contained in its true meaning, which have always been contained in it ever since it was written: to behold the lovely and bright displays of the divine perfections, and of the excellence and sufficiency of Christ, and the excellence and suitableness of the way of salvation by Christ, and the spiritual glory of the precepts and promises of Scripture, and so on. These things are, and always were, in the Bible, and would have been seen before if it had not been for blindness, without any new sense being added by the words’ being sent by God to a particular person and spoken to him anew, with a new meaning.

And as to a gracious #emph[leading of the Spirit,] it consists in two things: partly in #emph[instructing] a person in his duty by the Spirit, and partly in powerfully #emph[inducing] him to comply with that instruction. But so far as the gracious leading of the Spirit lies in instruction, it consists in a person’s being guided by a spiritual and distinguishing taste for what has true moral beauty in it. I have shown that spiritual knowledge consists primarily in a taste or relish of the loveliness and beauty of what is truly good and holy. This holy relish is a thing that discerns and distinguishes between good and evil, between holy and unholy, without going to the trouble of a train of reasoning. He who has a true relish of external beauty knows what is beautiful by looking at it. He has no need of a train of reasoning about the proportion of the features to determine whether what he sees is a beautiful face or not; he needs nothing but a glance of his eye. He who has a well-tuned musical ear knows whether the sound he hears is true harmony; he does not first need to go to the trouble of a mathematician’s reasonings about the proportion of the notes. He who has a sound palate knows what is good food as soon as he tastes it, without a physician’s reasoning about it. There is a holy beauty and sweetness in words and actions, as well as a natural beauty in faces and sounds, and sweetness in food. Job 12:11: “Does not the ear test words as the tongue tastes its food?” When a holy and lovely action is suggested to the thoughts of a holy soul, that soul, if it is in the lively exercise of its spiritual taste, at once sees a beauty in it, and so inclines to it and embraces it. On the contrary, if an unworthy, unholy action is suggested to it, its sanctified eye sees no beauty in it and is not pleased with it; its sanctified taste relishes no sweetness in it, but on the contrary finds it nauseous. Indeed, its holy taste and appetite lead it to think of what is truly lovely, and naturally suggest it, as a healthy taste and appetite naturally suggest the idea of their proper object. So a holy person is led by the Spirit as he is instructed and led by his holy taste and disposition of heart. By this, in the lively exercise of grace, he easily distinguishes good and evil, and knows at once what is suitable and lovely behavior toward God and toward man in this case and that. He judges what is right, as it were, spontaneously and of himself, without a particular deduction by any other arguments than the beauty that is seen and the goodness that is tasted. So Christ blames the #emph[Pharisees] because they #emph[did not, even of themselves, judge what was right,] without needing miracles to prove it (Luke 12:57). The apostle seems plainly to have this way of judging of spiritual beauty in view in Romans 12:2: “Be transformed by the renewing of your#super[+] mind. Then you#super[+] will be able to test and approve what is the good, pleasing, and perfect will of God.”

There is such a thing as a #emph[good taste] of #emph[natural] beauty (which learned men often speak of), exercised about #emph[temporal] things in judging them: about the justness of a speech, the goodness of a style, the beauty of a poem, the gracefulness of bearing, and so on. A late great philosopher of our nation writes this about it#footnote[#emph[Chambers]’s dictionary, under the word taste.]: To have #emph[a taste] is to give things their real value, to be touched with the good and shocked with the ill; not to be dazzled by false luster, but, in spite of all colors and everything that might deceive or mislead, to judge soundly. #emph[Taste] and #emph[judgment,] then, should be the same thing; and yet it is easy to see a difference. The #emph[judgment] forms its opinions from reflection. On this occasion reason takes a kind of roundabout way to arrive at its end: it supposes principles, it draws consequences, and it judges, but not without a thorough knowledge of the case; so that after it has pronounced, it is ready to give a reason for its decrees. #emph[Good taste] observes none of these formalities. Before it has time to consult, it has taken its side. As soon as the object is presented to it, the impression is made and the sentiment formed; ask no more of it. As the ear is wounded by a harsh sound, as the smell is soothed by an agreeable odor, before reason has meddled with those objects to judge them, so the #emph[taste] opens itself at once and goes before all reflection. Reflections may come afterward to confirm it, and show the secret reasons for its conduct; but it was not in its power to wait for them. Frequently it does not know them at all, and whatever pains it takes, it cannot discover what it was that determined it to think as it did. This conduct is very different from what the #emph[judgment] observes in its decisions; unless we choose to say that #emph[good taste] is, as it were, a first motion, or a kind of instinct of right reason, which hurries on with rapidity and leads more surely than all the reasonings she could make. It is a first glance of the eye, which shows us the nature and relations of things in a moment.

Now there is such a #emph[taste] of the mind as this, which philosophers speak of, by which persons are guided in judging the natural beauty, gracefulness, propriety, nobleness and sublimity of speeches and actions, judging, as it were, by the glance of the eye, or by inward sensation and the first impression of the object. So, likewise, there is such a thing as a #emph[divine taste,] given and maintained by the Spirit of God in the hearts of the saints, by which they are in the same way led and guided in discerning and distinguishing the true spiritual and holy beauty of actions; and they do so the more easily, readily and accurately as they have more or less of the Spirit of God dwelling in them. And so #emph[the sons of God are led by the Spirit of God in their behavior in the world.]

A holy disposition and spiritual taste, where grace is strong and lively, will enable a soul to determine what actions are right and becoming to Christians not only more quickly but far more exactly than the greatest abilities without it. This may be illustrated by the way some habits of mind and dispositions of heart, of a nature inferior to true grace, will teach and guide a man in his actions. For instance, if a man is a very good-natured man, his good nature will teach him better how to act benevolently among mankind, and will direct him on every occasion to the speeches and actions which agree with the rules of goodness, than the strongest reason will teach a man of a morose temper. So if a man’s heart is under the influence of an entire friendship and a most tender affection to another, then though he is a man of only ordinary ability, this habit of his mind will direct him far more readily and exactly to a speech and bearing, or manner of behavior, that will be in all respects sweet and kind and agreeable to a benevolent disposition of heart, than the greatest ability without it. He has, as it were, a spirit within him that guides him. The habit of his mind is accompanied by a taste by which he immediately relishes the air and manner that is benevolent and dislikes the contrary; and it causes him to distinguish between the one and the other #emph[in a moment,] more precisely than the most accurate reasonings can find out #emph[in many hours.] So the nature and inward tendency of a stone, or other heavy body, let fall from a height, shows the way to the center of the earth more exactly in an instant than the ablest mathematician without it could determine by his most accurate observations in a whole day. This is how a spiritual disposition and taste teaches and guides a man in his behavior in the world. So an eminently humble or meek or charitable disposition will direct a person of small ability to a behavior that agrees with the Christian rules of humility, meekness and charity far more readily and precisely than the most diligent study and elaborate reasonings of a man of the strongest faculties who does not have a Christian spirit within him. So too a spirit of love to God, of holy fear and reverence toward God, of childlike confidence in God, and a heavenly disposition, will teach and guide a man in his behavior.

It is an exceedingly difficult thing for a wicked man, who has no Christian principles in his heart to guide him, to know how to conduct himself like a Christian, with the life and beauty and heavenly sweetness of a truly holy, humble, Christlike behavior. He does not know how to put on these garments, and they do not fit him. Eccl 10:2–3: “A wise man’s heart inclines to the right, but the heart of a fool to the left. Even as the fool walks along the road, his sense is lacking, and he shows everyone that he is a fool.” With verse 15: “The toil of a fool wearies him, for he does not know the way to the city.” Prov 10:32: “The lips of the righteous know what is fitting.” Prov 15:2: “The tongue of the wise uses knowledge rightly, but the mouth of the fool spouts folly.” And Prov 16:23: “The heart of the wise man instructs his mouth and adds persuasiveness to his lips.”

The saints, in judging actions by a spiritual taste in this way, do not have particular recourse to the express rules of God’s word for every word and action before them whose good or evil they judge. Yet their taste itself in general is subject to the rule of God’s word, and must be tested by that, and by right reasoning on it. A man with a sound palate judges particular morsels by his taste; yet his palate itself must be judged, whether it is right or not, by certain rules and reasons. But a spiritual taste of soul mightily helps the soul in its reasonings on the word of God, and in judging the true meaning of its rules. It removes the prejudices of a depraved appetite, naturally leads the thoughts in the right channel, casts a light on the word of God, and causes the true meaning to come to mind most naturally, through the harmony there is between the disposition and relish of a sanctified soul and the true meaning of the rules of God’s word. Indeed, this harmony tends to bring the texts themselves to mind on proper occasions, as the particular state of the stomach and palate tends to bring to mind the particular foods and drinks that agree with that state. So #emph[the children of God are led by the Spirit of God] in judging actions themselves, and in their meditations on God’s holy word, and their judging and applying of its rules. And so God #emph[teaches them his statutes, and makes them understand the way of his precepts,] which the psalmist prays for so often.

But this leading of the Spirit is something exceedingly different from what some call by that name. Theirs consists not in teaching them God’s statutes and precepts, which he has already given, but in giving them new precepts by immediate inward speech or suggestion; and it has in it no tasting of the true excellence of things, nor any judging or discerning of the nature of things at all. They do not determine what the will of God is by any taste or relish, or by any kind of judgment of the nature of things, but by an immediate dictate concerning the thing to be done. There is no such thing as any judgment or wisdom in the case. Whereas in the leading of the Spirit which is peculiar to God’s children, that true wisdom and holy discretion so often spoken of in the word of God is imparted. It is as high above the other way as the stars are above a glowworm. It is what #emph[Balaam] and #emph[Saul] (who were at times led by the Spirit in that other way) never had, and what no natural man can have without a change of nature.

What has been said of the nature of spiritual understanding, as consisting most essentially in a divine, supernatural sense and relish of the heart, shows not only that there is nothing of it in this falsely supposed #emph[leading of the Spirit] just spoken of. It also shows the difference between spiritual understanding and all kinds and forms of enthusiasm: all imaginary sights of God and Christ and heaven; all supposed witnessing of the Spirit, and testimonies of the love of God by immediate inward suggestion; all impressions of future events, and immediate revelations of any secret facts whatever; all enthusiastic impressions and applications of words of Scripture, as though they were words now immediately spoken by God to a particular person, in a new meaning, and carrying something more in them than the words contain as they stand in the Bible; and all interpretations of the mystical meaning of Scripture by supposed immediate revelation. None of these things consists in a divine sense and relish of the #emph[heart] of the holy beauty and excellence of divine things, nor have they anything to do with such a sense. They all consist in impressions in the #emph[head.] They all belong under the head of #emph[impressions on the imagination,] and consist in stirring up external ideas in the mind: ideas of outward shapes and colors, or of words spoken or letters written, or ideas of external and sensible things belonging to actions done, or to events accomplished or to be accomplished. An enthusiastic supposed manifestation of the love of God is made by stirring up in the imagination an idea of a smiling face, or some other pleasant outward appearance, or the idea of pleasant words spoken or written, or some pleasant bodily sensation. So when persons have an imaginary revelation of some secret fact, it is by stirring up external ideas: either of some words implying a declaration of that fact, or of some visible or sensible circumstances of such a fact. So the supposed leading of the Spirit to do the will of God in outward behavior comes either by stirring up in their minds the idea of words (which are outward things), whether words of Scripture or other words, which they look on as an immediate command of God; or else by stirring up and strongly impressing the ideas of the outward actions themselves. So when an interpretation of a Scripture type or allegory is strongly suggested immediately, in an extraordinary way, it is by suggesting words, as though someone secretly whispered and told the meaning, or by stirring up other ideas in the imagination.

Such experiences and discoveries as these commonly raise the affections of those who are deluded by them to a great height, and make a mighty uproar in both soul and body. And a very great part of the false religion that has been in the world from one age to another consists in such discoveries as these and in the affections that flow from them. In such things consisted the experiences of the ancient #emph[Pythagoreans] among the heathen, and of many others among them, who had strange ecstasies and raptures and claimed a divine #emph[afflatus] \[inspiration\] and immediate revelations from heaven. In such things as these the experiences of the #emph[Essenes,] an ancient sect among the #emph[Jews] at and after the times of the apostles, seem to have consisted. In such things as these consisted the experiences of many of the ancient #emph[Gnostics] and #emph[Montanists,] and many other sects of ancient heretics in the early ages of the Christian church. And in such things as these consisted the claimed immediate converse with God and Christ, and with the saints and angels of heaven, of the #emph[monks, anchorites] and #emph[recluses] who formerly abounded in the church of #emph[Rome.] In such things consisted the claimed high experiences and great spirituality of many sects of enthusiasts that swarmed in the world after the Reformation: such as the #emph[Anabaptists, Antinomians] and #emph[Familists,] the followers of #emph[N. Stork, th. Muncer, Jo. Becold, Henry Pfeifer, David George, Casper Swenckfield, Henry Nicolas, Johannes Agricola Eislebius;] the many wild enthusiasts that were in #emph[England] in the days of #emph[Oliver Cromwell;] and the followers of Mrs. #emph[Hutchinson] in #emph[New England.] This appears from the particular and full accounts given of all these sects by that eminently holy man Mr. #emph[Samuel Rutherford] in his #emph[Display of the spiritual Antichrist.] And in such things as these consisted the experiences of the late #emph[French prophets] and their followers. And in these things the religion of the many kinds of enthusiasts of the present day seems to lie. It is chiefly by this sort of religion that #emph[Satan] transforms himself into an angel of light; and it is what he has always used most successfully to confound hopeful and happy revivals of religion, from the beginning of the Christian church to this day. When the Spirit of God is poured out to begin a glorious work, the old serpent, as fast as possible and by every means, brings in this bastard religion and mingles it with the true; and this has time after time soon brought everything into confusion. Its pernicious consequences are not easily imagined or conceived of until we see and are amazed by its fearful effects and the dismal desolation it has made. Even if the revival of true religion is very great in its beginning, yet if this bastard comes in, there is danger of its doing as #emph[Gideon]’s bastard #emph[Abimelech] did, who did not stop until he had killed all of Gideon’s seventy trueborn sons except one, who was forced to flee. Great and strict, then, should be the watch and guard ministers keep against such things, especially at a time of great awakening. For men, especially the common people, are easily bewitched by such things, since they have such a glaring and glittering show of high religion, and the devil hides his own shape and appears as an angel of light, so that men may not be afraid of him but may adore him.

The imagination or fancy seems to be where all those delusions of #emph[Satan] are formed which carry away those who are under the influence of false religion and counterfeit graces and affections. Here is the devil’s grand lurking-place, the very nest of foul and deluding spirits. It is very much to be doubted whether the devil can come at the soul of man at all to affect it, or to stir up any thought or motion, or produce any effect whatever in it, in any other way than by the fancy, which is that power of the soul by which it receives, and has in it, the images or ideas of outward and sensible things. As to the laws and means the Creator has established for the intercourse and communication of unbodied spirits, we know nothing about them. We do not know by what medium they make their thoughts known to each other, or stir up thoughts in each other. But as to spirits united to bodies, the bodies God has united them to are their medium of communication. They have no other medium of acting on other creatures, or of being acted on by them, than the body. So it is not to be supposed that #emph[Satan] can stir up any thought or produce any effect in the soul of man in any other way than by some motion of the animal spirits, or by causing some motion or alteration in something that belongs to the body. There is this reason to think that the devil cannot produce thoughts in the soul immediately, or in any other way than through the medium of the body: namely, that he cannot immediately see or know the thoughts of the soul. Scripture abundantly declares that to do that belongs to the omniscient God alone. But it is not likely that the devil can #emph[immediately produce] an effect which is out of the reach of his #emph[immediate view.] It seems unreasonable to suppose that his immediate agency should be out of his own sight, or that it should be impossible for him to see what he himself immediately does. Is it not unreasonable to suppose that any spirit or intelligent agent should, by the act of his will, produce effects according to his understanding, or agreeable to his own thoughts, and that immediately, and yet the effects produced should be beyond the reach of his understanding, where he can have no immediate perception or discerning at all? But if it is so that the devil cannot produce thoughts in the soul immediately, or in any other way than by the animal spirits, or by the body, then it follows that he never brings anything about in the soul except by the imagination or fancy, or by stirring up external ideas. For we know that alterations in the body immediately stir up no other sort of ideas in the mind than external ideas, or ideas of the outward senses, or ideas of the same outward nature. As to reflection, abstraction, reasoning and so on, and the thoughts and inward motions which are the fruits of these acts of the mind, they are not the direct effects of impressions on the body. So it must be only by the imagination that #emph[Satan] has access to the soul to tempt and delude it, or to suggest anything to it. #footnote[The imagination is that room of the soul in which the devil often appears. Indeed (to speak exactly) the devil has no efficient power over the rational part of a man. He cannot change the will; he cannot alter the heart of a man. So the utmost he can do in tempting a man to sin is by persuasion and suggestion only. But then how does the devil do this? By working on the imagination. He observes the temper and bodily constitution of a man, and accordingly suggests things to his fancy and throws his fiery darts into it, and by these the mind and will come to be worked on. So the devil has no commanding efficacy over your will; yet because he can stir and move your imagination in this way, and you, being naturally without grace, cannot withstand these suggestions, it comes about that any sin in your imagination, though only in the outward works of the soul, quickly lays hold on all. And indeed, it is by this means that those horrible delusions arise which are found in many erroneous ways of religion: it is all because their imaginations are corrupted. Indeed, how often are these devilish delusions of the imagination taken for the gracious operations of God’s Spirit?—It is from this that many have laid claim to enthusiasms;—they leave the Scriptures and attend wholly to what they perceive and feel within them. #emph[Burgess] on original sin, p. 369. The great #emph[Turretine,] speaking on the question #emph[What is the power of angels,] says: As to bodies, there is no doubt that they can do a great deal with all sorts of elementary and sublunary bodies, to move them from place to place and to agitate them in various ways. It is also certain that they can act on the external and internal senses, to stir them up or to bind them. But as to the rational soul itself, they can do nothing immediately on it; for it belongs to God alone, who knows and searches the hearts and has them in his hands, also to bend and move them wherever he wills. But angels can act on the rational soul only mediately, by imaginations. #emph[Theolog. Elench. Loc.] VII #emph[quest. 7.]] And this seems to be the reason why persons suffering from the disease of melancholy are commonly so visibly and remarkably subject to the suggestions and temptations of #emph[Satan.] For that is a disease which especially affects the animal spirits, and is accompanied by weakness of the part of the body which is the fountain of the animal spirits, namely the brain, which is, as it were, the seat of the fancy. It is by impressions made on the brain that any ideas are stirred up in the mind by the motion of the animal spirits, or by any changes made in the body. When the brain is weakened and diseased in this way, it is less under the command of the higher faculties of the soul, yields more easily to impressions from outside, and is overpowered by the disordered motions of the animal spirits; and so the devil has a greater advantage to affect the mind by working on the imagination. And so when #emph[Satan] casts those horrid suggestions into the minds of many melancholy persons, in which they have no hand themselves, he does it by stirring up imaginary ideas, either of some dreadful words or sentences or of other horrid outward ideas. And when he tempts other persons who are not melancholy, he does it by presenting to the imagination, in a lively and alluring way, the objects of their lusts; or by stirring up ideas of words, and so by them stirring up thoughts; or by promoting an imagination of outward actions, events, circumstances and so on. Countless are the ways in which the mind might be led on to all kinds of evil thoughts by stirring up external ideas in the imagination.

If persons keep no guard at these avenues of #emph[Satan,] by which he has access to the soul to tempt and delude it, they are likely to have enough of him. And especially so if, instead of guarding against him, they lay themselves open to him, and seek and invite him, because he appears as an angel of light and counterfeits the illuminations and graces of the Spirit of God by inward whispers and immediate suggestions of facts and events, pleasant voices, beautiful images and other impressions on the imagination. There are many who are deluded by such things, are lifted up by them and seek after them, who have a continual course of them and can have them almost whenever they want; and especially when their pride and vainglory most need them, to make a show of them before company. It is with them something as it is with those who practice the art of telling where lost things are to be found by impressions made on their imaginations: since they lay themselves open to the devil, he is always at hand to give them the impression they desire.

Before I finish what I would say on this head of imaginations counterfeiting spiritual light, and the affections arising from them, I would again ask (to prevent any misunderstanding of what has been said) that it be observed that I am far from concluding that no affections are spiritual which are accompanied by imaginary ideas. Such is the nature of man that he can hardly think of anything intensely without some kind of outward ideas. They arise and put themselves in the way unavoidably in the course of a man’s thoughts, though they are often very confused and are not what the mind attends to. When the mind is much engaged and the thoughts intense, the imagination is often stronger and the outward idea more lively, especially in persons of certain bodily constitutions. But there is a great difference between these two things: #emph[lively imaginations arising from strong affections,] and #emph[strong affections arising from lively imaginations.] The former may happen, and no doubt often does, in the case of truly gracious affections. The affections do not arise from the imagination, nor depend on it at all; on the contrary, the imagination is only the accidental effect or consequence of the affection, through the weakness of human nature. But when the latter is the case, as it often is, that the affection arises from the imagination and is built on it as its foundation, instead of on a spiritual illumination or discovery, then the affection, however elevated, is worthless and vain. And this is the purpose of what has now been said about impressions on the imagination. Having observed this, I proceed to another mark of gracious affections.
